nat44-ei: hairpinning on a LAN that is not an inside interface

Hairpinning (a LAN host reaching a port forward through the router's own
outside address) needs in2out on the LAN, but with the output feature on
the exits, as the installer configures NAT, making the LAN inside
translates all its traffic twice: once on input and again in the exit's
output feature, whose worker handoff then sends everything to one worker
because every source is the one outside address. On the test router that
cut NAT throughput from 6.8 to 3.0 Mpps.

Add "set interface nat44 ei hairpinning <intfc> [del]" (and "show nat44
ei hairpinning interfaces"). It puts nat44-ei-hairpin-classify on the
interface's ip4-unicast arc, after input ACLs, reassembly, dns-intercept
and ABF. Only TCP and UDP whose destination address and port hairpinning
can resolve, an out2in session (which also catches the inside service's
replies) or a static mapping other than an identity mapping, go to
nat44-ei-in2out (or its worker handoff); everything else, including other
traffic to the outside address, continues untouched. In2out then rewrites
the source and, with 0039, ip4-local's nat44-ei-hairpinning the
destination, as for an inside interface. EI in2out always continues to
ip4-lookup, so no next-feature index crosses node lists. ICMP and
non-first fragments are not hairpinned.
Destinations are first compared with a small array of the NAT addresses
(pool addresses and non-identity static mappings' external addresses), so
other traffic needs no hash lookup. The array is rebuilt at the end of
nat44_ei_add_address, nat44_ei_del_address, the static mapping add and
delete, and plugin disable. Address changes from DHCP can run without the
barrier, so the count is cleared first and set last: a worker reading
during a rebuild can at worst misclassify one packet, which in2out or the
next feature still handles. With more than 64 addresses every TCP/UDP
packet is looked up. Enabling it on an inside interface
is refused. Only the NAT plugin changes; nat44_ei_main_t is unchanged.

Verified in an isolated VPP with the output feature on the outside
interface and hairpinning on the inside one: hairpinned TCP connects, UDP
gets its answer from the mapped port, outbound traffic, its replies and an
ABF redirect are unaffected, and checksums stay right with and without
Linux checksum offload. On the test router (2 workers, LAN as bridge BVI,
WAN output feature, Gate's DNS hijack, a WireGuard and a bridged exit) the
hijack, every exit and hairpinning worked; TRex 64-byte UDP NAT throughput
was unchanged up to 6 Mpps and saturated at 6.2 instead of 6.8 Mpps, the
cost of two or three hash lookups per TCP/UDP packet. Test:
tests/integration/integration_nat44_partial_checksum.py --nat ei --mode hairpin.

